-Note that the query and `create-sql` replacements are currently heuristics and can go wrong. If possible, share an unanonymized workload yaml file.
+#### The `mz-sql-anonymize` helper
+
+The anonymizer drives a standalone Rust binary, `mz-sql-anonymize` (crate `src/sql-anonymize`), as a subprocess over a JSON protocol. It reads a JSON request on stdin and writes a JSON response on stdout, rewriting each statement on the parsed AST. It is located via the `MZ_SQL_ANONYMIZE_BIN` environment variable if set, otherwise from `target/release/mz-sql-anonymize` or `target/debug/mz-sql-anonymize` in the repository. Build it with:
+
+```bash
+cargo build -p mz-sql-anonymize
+```
+
+#### Built-in keep-lists
+
+Two allowlists in `test/workload-replay/` name built-in identifiers that are never renamed, because renaming them breaks replay:
+
+- `system_catalog_identifiers.txt` — built-in object, column, function, and type names (e.g. a view over `mz_internal.mz_role_auth` must keep `password_hash`)
+- `load_generator_identifiers.txt` — built-in load-generator output columns (e.g. `l_quantity`, `c_acctbal`)
+
+Each file's header records the SQL it was generated from.
 
